1
00:00:00,270 --> 00:00:04,203
Sarah reports duplicate follow-up tasks after ordinary edits.

2
00:00:04,523 --> 00:00:06,922
You helped implement the recent Flow change.

3
00:00:07,242 --> 00:00:11,275
In this fictional incident, the automation responds while a record

4
00:00:11,275 --> 00:00:15,167
remains ready, instead of only when it enters the intended

5
00:00:15,167 --> 00:00:15,750
state.

6
00:00:16,831 --> 00:00:21,718
Identify affected records, users, timing, and operational consequences.

7
00:00:22,037 --> 00:00:26,058
Preserve useful evidence and notify Maya promptly with what is

8
00:00:26,058 --> 00:00:27,762
known and uncertain.

9
00:00:28,107 --> 00:00:31,715
Quietly changing production to hide embarrassment can make both the

10
00:00:31,715 --> 00:00:34,540
technical problem and the trust problem worse.

11
00:00:35,572 --> 00:00:40,167
Work with authorized owners on containment, rollback, or recovery.

12
00:00:40,470 --> 00:00:43,340
A faulty automation might need to be paused, but the

13
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:47,259
choice depends on scope, alternatives, and the approved process.

14
00:00:47,587 --> 00:00:49,848
Avoid deleting tasks indiscriminately;

15
00:00:50,342 --> 00:00:52,399
some may represent legitimate work.

16
00:00:53,453 --> 00:00:58,366
In a suitable test environment, recreate initial handoff, later edits,

17
00:00:58,505 --> 00:01:00,505
repeated events, and reopening.

18
00:01:00,837 --> 00:01:04,397
Compare the intended transition with the actual trigger behavior.

19
00:01:04,690 --> 00:01:09,809
Correct the conditions and duplicate prevention, then verify connected workflows.

20
00:01:10,868 --> 00:01:14,475
Add a regression case covering edits while the record remains

21
00:01:14,475 --> 00:01:15,701
in the same state.

22
00:01:16,024 --> 00:01:18,982
Document the gap, correction, and recovery.

23
00:01:19,319 --> 00:01:23,329
Explain directly that the earlier tests missed this scenario and

24
00:01:23,329 --> 00:01:25,453
how the testing process has changed.

25
00:01:26,503 --> 00:01:30,137
Owning a mistake does not erase its consequences, especially for

26
00:01:30,137 --> 00:01:31,428
serious incidents.

27
00:01:31,735 --> 00:01:38,372
Transparent investigation, appropriate escalation, careful correction, and reduced recurrence are

28
00:01:38,372 --> 00:01:39,148
essential.

29
00:01:39,467 --> 00:01:43,926
The professional objective is dependable behavior when problems occur, not

30
00:01:43,926 --> 00:01:46,042
a claim of perfect performance.
